ute over the  astrazeneca vaccines i'm joined now by  our man in brussels george martinez good  evening to you  georg there is a lot of um blaming and  finger pointing across both sides of the  english channel  who is at fault here the eu the uk or is  it astrazeneca  uh clearly astrazeneca has not been able  to keep promises  it has made that i think that's a fact  then the uk obviously made a lot of good  pragmatic decisions but also  they had the quantities they had the  vaccines and a lot of those vaccines  as we have heard uh have come from the  european union not just  by not just astrazeneca but also  bioentech pfizer and madonna 10 million  doses  what the eu was not aware of or what  they did not calculate in their strategy  is  that the uk had a uk first clause  in their contract so one way of looking  at it is i think  is to say that the eu was a bit too  naive was  trusting too much into open markets in  their strategy  of getting vaccines yeah that's a good  way to put it and we know today the  european union announced tougher export  rules on vaccines  are are we on the verge of what could be  a period of vaccine nationalism  i'd say that is exactly what the  brussels institutions hope to avoid  and in that light you also have to  understand that legislation that has  been put forward by the commission now  to restrict uh the mechanism that  observes  exports of of vaccines it is not just a  deterrent  for countries uh to play by the rules  but it's also an  incentive and in that light you could  also see  a joint statement by the european union  and the uk  that has just been published talking  about creating a win-win situation  working together hand in hand on  openness and global cooperation  all right dw's gabriel matas with the  ladies tonight from brussels  gee york thank you
 

